 "docs": {
  "en": {
   "title": "Crested Butte ski resort snow report: 38 in up top (US West, 18 November)",
   "description": "Crested Butte has 38 in (97 cm) on upper slopes on 18 November. Telluride leads the region with 50 in (126 cm). Snow covers 281,920 sq mi across the US West.",
   "lede": "Crested Butte holds 38 in (97 cm) on its upper slopes today, putting it level with Cimarron Mountain Club but well behind Telluride's 50 in (126 cm). At mid-mountain (10,285 ft / 3,135 m), Crested Butte sits at 7 in (19 cm)—below the usual 12 in (31 cm) for this date—so early-season skiers need to pick their lines with care.",
   "sections": [
    {
     "h": "Where the snow sits at Crested Butte",
     "p": [
      "Snow depth on Crested Butte spreads unevenly by height. At the base (9,125 ft / 2,783 m) almost nothing lies on the ground: just 1 in (3 cm). The mid-mountain zone (10,285 ft / 3,135 m) has 7 in (19 cm). The top (11,443 ft / 3,488 m) reaches 14 in (36 cm).",
      "This vertical split is typical for November: cold air sits high, so snow persists above 10,000 ft but melts or never accumulates lower down. At Crested Butte on this date, only terrain above mid-mountain is reliable for skiing."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Behind normal for mid-November",
     "p": [
      "At 10,285 ft (3,135 m), Crested Butte usually holds 12 in (31 cm) on 18 November. This year the depth is 7 in (19 cm)—only 15 of the past 46 winters on this date held less snow at this height. The shortfall is not dramatic, but it means rocks and grass show through where snow is thin.",
      "In contrast, Telluride at the same height has 22 in (56 cm), nearly double Crested Butte's total."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Rain and freeze cycle ahead",
     "p": [
      "The freezing level on 18 November reaches as high as 5,085 ft (1,550 m) in the warmest part of the day, then drops to 3,610 ft (1,100 m) on 20 November. This swing means wet snow at mid-elevations now, then refreezing later in the week.",
      "Fresh snow is coming: 4 in (10 cm) through 21 November, mostly on 21 November itself. That boost will help, but only the top slopes will stay dry for the next few days."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Telluride and Cimarron lead the region",
     "p": [
      "Telluride sits at the top of the US West with 50 in (126 cm) on its upper slopes. Cimarron Mountain Club and Crested Butte tie at 38 in (97 cm). Wolf Creek follows at 36 in (93 cm), and Brian Head Resort holds 36 in (92 cm).",
      "Across the entire region, 168 of 193 resorts have snow on their upper slopes, covering about 282,000 sq km. That is a dramatic jump from just 104,348 sq mi (270,260 km²) only two days earlier."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "The bigger picture: depth versus water content",
     "p": [
      "Snow depth alone does not tell the full story. A thin layer of dense, settled snow holds more water than a fluffy new dump of the same depth. The measured depths at nearby stations offer a reality check: Beartown (11,581 ft / 3,530 m) reported 33 in (84 cm) on 16 November, Black Mesa at the same height recorded 27 in (69 cm), and Leavitt Lake (9,600 ft / 2,926 m) had 28 in (71 cm) with 10 in (25 cm) falling in one day.",
      "These stations confirm that early-season snow at Crested Butte is settling and thinning at lower elevations. The map shows what the snowpack looks like right now; check it again before you travel."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "What to expect through the week",
     "p": [
      "Crested Butte will gain roughly 4 in (10 cm) through 21 November: nothing today or on 19 November, 1 in (3 cm) on 20 November, and 3 in (7 cm) on 21 November. These amounts can still shift; forecasts a few days out are less certain than today's picture.",
      "Snow at the base of Crested Butte will remain thin and patchy. High, north-facing slopes above 11,000 ft (3,350 m) will collect the most fresh snow and hold it longest."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "advice": [
    "Pick your runs high: 11,443 ft (3,488 m) has 14 in (36 cm) today. Avoid the base and mid-mountain slopes if you do not want to ski over rocks or dirt.",
    "Watch for refrozen surfaces after the warm spell today. Icy patches are likely on steep terrain early in the morning after tonight's freeze.",
    "The forecast adds 4 in (10 cm) through 21 November, mostly on 21 November. Wait until then if you are hoping for fresh tracks and better coverage.",
    "Before you leave the piste or go touring, check the Colorado Avalanche Information Center bulletin for Crested Butte. Early-season slides can be unpredictable on thin snowpack.",
    "Early-season snow is thin. Rocks hide just under the surface. Stick to open, groomed terrain until snow depth is reliable across all elevations."
   ],
   "captions": {
    "map": "Snow depth across the US West on 18 November 2026, showing Crested Butte with 38 in (97 cm) on upper slopes and Telluride leading the region at 50 in (126 cm)."
   }
  }
